  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Pleasant/dry pattern continues into the last\nweekend of July.\n\nAfter a stormy period since last weekend, a really nice\nafternoon outside with clear skie and low humidity levels.\nPW values about as low as they get this time of year.\n\nHigh pressure over the region will provide the area with \nanother night with clear skies and cool temperatures.\n\nFriday will feature another day of sunshine and low humidity.\n\nWhile there could be a shower or storm over the region this \nweekend, the airmass remains rather dry. Main support for any\nactivity would be across western sections on Sunday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Turning more humid with showers and storms next \nweek. Perhaps a few strong storms with heavy rain at times.\n\nWeak systems dropping southeast next week will bring chances\nfor showers and storms at times. Systems are rather weak and\nwill be moving along, so timing would be a factor.\n\nThe other factor is that the higher dewpoint air is expected \nto be rather limited, mainly later on Tuesday. \n\nMain timing for anything widespread would be late Monday night\ninto early Wednesday. \n\nMore detail below.\n\nThe pattern looks increasingly unsettled Sunday into the middle\nof next week as upper troughing is carved out over the Great\nLakes/Ohio Valley into the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast states. \n\nAmple northern stream system energies set to dig into the upper \ntrough position will support potential for strong storms and\nheavy rainfall. WPC introduced a broad level 1/4 marginal \nexcessive rain risk for D5/Monday July 27 that covers most of \nCPA. \n\nSevere storm potential could shift east/southeast on Tuesday. \nConfidence in where greater risk corridors may develop is low, \nand will largely be influenced by convective evolution in the \nDays 4-5/Sunday-Monday periods.",
